final_output:  ["194,192.\n\nWait, but let me check again. When I multiply 212 by 900, that should be 212*9*100 = 1908*100 = 190,800. Then adding 1272 + 2120 gives 3,392. Adding that to 190,800: 190,800 + 3,392 = 194,192. Hmm, but maybe I made a mistake in shifting. Let me try a different method:\n\nAlternatively, 212 × 916 can be calculated as:\n\n212 × (900 + 16) = 212×900 + 212×16.\n\n212×900 = 190,800.\n\n212×16: 212×10=2,120; 212×6=1,272. So 2,120 + 1,272 = 3,392.\n\nAdding together: 190,800 + 3,392 = 194,192. Same result. So maybe correct?\n\nWait, but let me do the multiplication step by step properly:\n\nMultiplying 212 by each digit of 916:\n\nFirst, write 212 × 916:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n- Units digit: 6 × 212 = 1,272.\n\n- Tens digit: 1 (in tens place is 10) so 10 × 212 = 2,120.\n\n- Hundreds digit: 9 (in hundreds place is 900) so 900 × 212 = 190,800.\n\nAdd them: 1,272 + 2,120 = 3,392; 3,392 + 190,800 = 194,192.\n\nYes, that's consistent. Alternatively, using another method:\n\n212 × 916:\n\nLet me compute 200×900 = 180,000; 200×16 = 3,200; 12×900=10,800; 12×16=192.\n\nAdding all together: 180,000 + 3,200 + 10,800 + 192.\n\n180,000 + 3,200 = 183,200; 183,200 + 10,800 = 194,000; 194,000 + 192 = 194,192. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, use the standard vertical multiplication:\n\n       212\n\n     ×916\n\n     --------\n\n       1272  (212 × 6)\n\n      2120   (212 × 1, shifted one position left)\n\n    189600  (212 × 9, shifted two positions left)\n\n    --------\n\n    Sum these:\n\n    1272 + 2120 = 3392\n\n    3392 + 189,600 = 192,992? Wait, wait, hold on! Wait, this is different.\n\nWait, hold on, there's an inconsistency here. Wait in the first calculation, when I did 212 × 9 (hundreds place), I considered it as 900, so 212 × 900 = 190,800. But in this vertical multiplication, the third line is 189,600? That can't be.\n\nWait, let me recalculate 212 × 9 (hundreds digit):\n\nWait, the hundreds digit is 9, so it's 9×212, but shifted two places (because it's the hundreds place). So 9×212 = 1,908, then shifted two places is 190,800. But in the vertical multiplication above, the third line was written as 189,600, which is incorrect. So that was a mistake in my initial step-by-step. So let me redo that properly.\n\nLet me write the vertical multiplication correctly:\n\n       212\n\n     × 916\n\n     --------\n\n       1272   (212 × 6)\n\n      2120    (212 × 1, shifted one position left → ×10)\n\n     190800   (212 × 9, shifted two positions left → ×100)\n\n     ---------\n\n     Adding them: 1272 + 2120 = 3392; 3392 + 190,800 = 194,192. Correct.\n\nAh, so my mistake was in the previous vertical step where I wrote 189,600 instead of 190,800. So the correct total is 194,192.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute 212 × 916 using another method to confirm.\n\nCompute 200 × 900 = 180,000.\n\n200 × 16 = 3,200.\n\n12 × 900 = 10,800.\n\n12 × 16 = 192.\n\nAdding all parts:\n\n180,000 + 3,200 = 183,200.\n\n183,200 + 10,800 = 194,000.\n\n194,000 + 192 = 194,192. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, using distributive property:\n\n212 × 916 = 212 × (900 + 16) = 212×900 + 212×16 = 190,800 + 3,392 = 194,192.\n\nAlternatively, use calculator verification:\n\n212 × 916:\n\nLet me compute 212 × 900 = 190,800.\n\n212 × 16: 212×10=2,120; 212×6=1,272 → 2,120+1,272=3,392.\n\nTotal: 190,800 + 3,392 = 194,192.\n\nAll methods lead to 194,192. Therefore, the product of 212 and 916 is 194,192.\n\nBut wait, the user might expect a more straightforward step-by-step explanation without the initial confusion. Let me present the correct steps clearly.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Multiply 212 by the units digit of 916 (6):**\n   \\[\n   212 \\times 6 = 1,272\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 212 by the tens digit of 916 (1), shifted one position to the left (equivalent to ×10):**\n   \\[\n   212 \\times 10 = 2,120\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 212 by the hundreds digit of 916 (9), shifted two positions to the left (equivalent to ×100):**\n   \\[\n   212 \\times 900 = 190,800\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add all the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   1,272 + 2,120 + 190,800 = 194,192\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:** The product of 212 and 916 is \\boxed{194192}.\n\nWait, but the user might have wanted the answer in a different format. Let me check if there is any miscalculation again.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute 212 × 916 directly:\n\nFirst, 212 × 916:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n212 × 900 = 190,800\n\n212 × 16 = 3,392\n\nTotal = 190,800 + 3,392 = 194,192.\n\nAlternatively, 916 × 200 = 183,200; 916 × 12 = 10,992. Then total is 183,200 + 10,992 = 194,192. Correct.\n\nYes, all methods confirm the answer is 194,192. So the final answer is correct. The initial confusion was due to a miscalculation in one of the vertical steps, but upon rechecking, it's consistent.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 212 and 916 is \\boxed{194192}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 212 and 916, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 212 by the units digit of 916 (6):**\n   \\[\n   212 \\times 6 = 1272\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 212 by the tens digit of 916 (1), shifted one position to the left (equivalent to ×10):**\n   \\[\n   212 \\times 10 = 2120\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 212 by the hundreds digit of 916 (9), shifted two positions to the left (equivalent to ×100):**\n   \\[\n   212 \\times 900 = 190800\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add all the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   1272 + 2120 + 190800 = 194192\n   \\]\n\n**Step-by-Step Verification:**\n- First, add the results of the units and tens place multiplications:\n  \\[\n  1272 + 2120 = 3392\n  \\]\n- Then add this result to the hundreds place multiplication:\n  \\[\n  3392 + 190800 = 194192\n  \\]\n\nThus, the product of 212 and 916 is \\boxed{194192}.", '']  3%|2         | 14/500 [1:29:26<46:12:27, 342.28s/it]
