      "text": "535392. <\uff5c/Assistant\uff5c>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 858 and 624. Hmm, let me think about the best way to approach this. I remember that multiplying larger numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm?\n\nAlright, let's start by writing down the numbers vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a0858  \n\u00d7\u00a0624  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nFirst, I should multiply 858 by each digit of 624, starting from the rightmost digit, which is 4. Let me do that step by step.\n\nMultiplying 858 by 4:\n8 times 4 is 32, so write down 2 and carry over 3.\n5 times 4 is 20, plus the carried 3 makes 23. Write down 3 and carry over 2.\n8 times 4 is 32, plus the carried 2 is 34. So that gives 3432. Okay, so the first partial product is 3432.\n\nNext, I need to multiply 858 by the tens place, which is 2. But since it's the tens place, it's actually 20. So I have to shift the result one position to the left. Let me do that:\n\nMultiplying 858 by 2 (which represents 20):\n8 times 2 is 16, write down 6 and carry over 1.\n5 times 2 is 10, plus 1 is 11. Write down 1 and carry over 1.\n8 times 2 is 16, plus 1 is 17. So that gives 1716, but since it's the tens place, it becomes 17160. Got that.\n\nNow, moving on to the hundreds place, which is 6. But since it's the hundreds place, it's actually 600. So we'll multiply 858 by 6 and then shift two places to the left.\n\nMultiplying 858 by 6 (which represents 600):\n8 times 6 is 48, write down 8 and carry over 4.\n5 times 6 is 30, plus 4 is 34. Write down 4 and carry over 3.\n8 times 6 is 48, plus 3 is 51. So that gives 5148, but shifted two places becomes 514800. Alright.\n\nNow, I need to add up all these partial products:\n\nFirst partial product: \u00a0\u00a03432  \nSecond partial product: 17160  \nThird partial product: 514800  \n\nAdding them together. Let me line them up properly:\n\n\u2003\u20033432  \n\u200317160  \n+514800  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStarting from the rightmost digits: 2 + 0 + 0 = 2.  \nNext digit: 3 + 6 + 0 = 9.  \nNext: 4 + 1 + 8 = 13. Write down 3 and carry over 1.  \nNext: 3 (from 3432) + 7 (from 17160) + 4 (from 514800) plus the carried 1. Wait, let me check the alignment again.\n\nWait, maybe I should write them all out aligned by place:\n\n\u2003\u00a0\u00a03432  \n\u2003\u00a017160  \n+514800  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nLet me write them vertically properly:\n\nStarting from the right:\nUnits place: 2 (from 3432), 0 (from 17160), 0 (from 514800) \u2192 2  \nTens place: 3 (from 3432), 6 (from 17160), 0 \u2192 3+6=9  \nHundreds place: 4 (from 3432), 1 (from 17160), 8 (from 514800) \u2192 4+1+8=13. Write 3, carryover 1.  \nThousands place: 3 (from 3432), 7 (from 17160), 4 (from 514800) plus the carryover 1 \u2192 3+7+4+1=15. Write 5, carryover 1.  \nTen-thousands place: 0 (since 3432 has no digit here), 1 (from 17160), 1 (from 514800, since 514800's digit here is 1 in the ten-thousands place?) Wait, let me check the numbers again.\n\nWait, 514800 is 5 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (units). So the digits:\n\n514800:  \nDigit positions:  \nHundred thousands:5, Ten thousands:1, Thousands:4, Hundreds:8, Tens:0, Units:0.\n\n17160:  \nTen thousands:1 (since it's 17,160), thousands:7, hundreds:1, tens:6, units:0.\n\n3432:  \nThousands:3, hundreds:4, tens:3, units:2.\n\nSo when adding:\n\nLet me write all the numbers with their digits:\n\n3432:  \nUnits:2  \nTens:3  \nHundreds:4  \nThousands:3  \nTen-thousands:0  \nHundred-thousands:0  \n\n17160:  \nUnits:0  \nTens:6  \nHundreds:1  \nThousands:7  \nTen-thousands:1  \nHundred-thousands:0  \n\n514800:  \nUnits:0  \nTens:0  \nHundreds:8  \nThousands:4  \nTen-thousands:1  \nHundred-thousands:5  \n\nSo adding column by column:\n\nStarting from the right (units):\n\nUnits: 2 + 0 + 0 = 2  \nTens: 3 + 6 + 0 = 9  \nHundreds: 4 + 