  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "349,530.\n\n<\uff5cbegin{solution}\uff5c>.\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\nTo find the product of 955 and 366, we can use the distributive property by breaking down each number into its place values and then multiplying each part individually before adding the results together.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers:**\n   - 955 can be decomposed into \\(900 + 50 + 5\\).\n   - 366 can be decomposed into \\(300 + 60 + 6\\).\n\n2. **Apply the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   (900 + 50 + 5) \\times (300 + 60 + 6)\n   \\]\n   This requires multiplying each component of the first number by each component of the second number.\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product:**\n   - Multiply \\(900\\) by each part of 366:\n     - \\(900 \\times 300 = 270,000\\)\n     - \\(900 \\times 60 = 54,000\\)\n     - \\(900 \\times 6 = 5,400\\)\n   - Multiply \\(50\\) by each part of 366:\n     - \\(50 \\times 300 = 15,000\\)\n     - \\(50 \\times 60 = 3,000\\)\n     - \\(50 \\times 6 = 300\\)\n   - Multiply \\(5\\) by each part of 366:\n     - \\(5 \\times 300 = 1,500\\)\n     - \\(5 \\times 60 = 300\\)\n     - \\(5 \\times 6 = 30\\)\n\n4. **Sum all the partial products:**\n   - Add the results step-by-step:\n     - Start with \\(270,000 + 54,000 = 324,000\\)\n     - \\(324,000 + 5,400 = 329,400\\)\n     - \\(329,400 + 15,000 = 344,400\\)\n     - \\(344,400 + 3,000 = 347,400\\)\n     - \\(347,400 + 300 = 347,700\\)\n     - \\(347,700 + 1,500 = 349,200\\)\n     - \\(349,200 + 300 = 349,500\\)\n     - \\(349,500 + 30 = 349,530\\)\n\n5. **Verification using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 955 by 6 (units place): \\(955 \\times 6 = 5,730\\)\n   - Multiply 955 by 60 (tens place): \\(955 \\times 60 = 57,300\\)\n   - Multiply 955 by 300 (hundreds place): \\(955 \\times 300 = 286,500\\)\n   - Add these results: \\(5,730 + 57,300 = 63,030\\), then \\(63,030 + 286,500 = 349,530\\)\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 955 and 366 is **349,530**.\n\n<\uff5cend{solution}\uff5c>. <\uff5cbegin{answer}\uff5c>.\nThe product of 955 and 366 is \\boxed{349530}.\n<\uff5cend{answer}\uff5c>.\n<\uff5cUser\uff5c>\nOkay, so I need to figure out the product of 955 and 366. Hmm, let's see. I remember from school that multiplying large numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe using the distributive property? Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I should break down both numbers into their hundreds, tens, and ones. Let's start with 955. Breaking that down, it's 900 + 50 + 5. Right? Because 900 is the hundreds place, 50 is the tens, and 5 is the ones. \n\nNow 366. That would be 300 + 60 + 6. Yep, same logic here. 300 for the hundreds, 60 for the tens, and 6 for the ones.\n\nSo, if I use the distributive property, it's like (900 + 50 + 5) multiplied by (300 + 60 + 6). That should give me all the combinations of each part multiplied together. Let me write that out:\n\n(900 + 50 + 5) \u00d7 (300 + 60 + 6) \n\nExpanding this, I need to multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number. That sounds like a lot of terms, but maybe manageable. Let me list them all out.\n\nStarting with the 900 part:\n- 900 \u00d7 300 = ?\nHmm, 900 times 300. Well, 9 times 3 is 27, and then adding four zeros (since 900 is 9\u00d7100 and 300 is 3\u00d7100, so total 100\u00d7100 = 10,000). So 27\u00d710,000 = 270,000. Okay, that's straightforward.\n\nNext, 900 \u00d7 60. Let's see, 900 times 60. 9\u00d76 is 54, and then three zeros (since 100\u00d710 = 1000). So 54,000.\n\nThen, 900 \u00d7 6. That should be 900\u00d76. 9\u00d76 is 54, so 54\u00d7100 = 5,400. Got that.\n\nNow moving on to the 50 part:\n- 50 \u00d7 300. 5\u00d73 is 15, so 15\u00d7100\u00d710 = 15,000? Wait, no. Wait, 50\u00d7300 is 50\u00d73\u00d7100. 50\u00d73 is 150, so 150\u00d7100 is 15,000. Right.\n\n- 50 \u00d7 60. That's 50\u00d760. 5\u00d76=30, and then two zeros (since 50 is 5\u00d710 and 60 is 6\u00d710, so 10\u00d710=100). So 30\u00d7100=3,000.\n\n- 50 \u00d7 6. 50\u00d76 is 300. Yeah, 5\u00d76=30, so 30\u00d710=300.\n\nNow the 5 part:\n- 5 \u00d7 300. 5\u00d7300 is 1,500. Because 5\u00d73=15, so 15\u00d7100=1,500.\n\n- 5 \u00d7 60. That's 5\u00d760=300. 5\u00d76=30, so 30\u00d710=300.\n\n- 5 \u00d7 6. That's just 30. Easy enough.\n\nAlright, so now I have all the partial products. Let me list them again to make sure I didn't miss any:\n\nFrom 900:\n- 270,000\n- 54,000\n- 5,400\n\nFrom 50:\n- 15,000\n- 3,000\n- 300\n\nFrom 5:\n- 1,500\n- 300\n- 30\n\nNow I need to add all these together. Let me start adding step by step to avoid mistakes.\n\nStarting with the first one: 270,000.\n\nAdd 54,000: 270,000 + 54,000 = 324,000.\n\nNext add 5,400: 324,000 + 5,400 = 329,400.\n\nNow add 15,000: 329,400 + 15,000 = 344,400.\n\nAdding 3,000: 344,400 + 3,000 = 347,400.\n\nThen 300: 347,400 + 300 = 347,700.\n\nNext, 1,500: 347,700 + 1,500 = 349,200.\n\nAdding another 300: 349,200 + 300 = 349,500.\n\nFinally, add the last 30: 349,500 + 30 = 349,530.\n\nHmm, so that gives me 349,530. Let me verify this another way to make sure I didn't make an addition error.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's try that method to cross-check.\n\nMultiplying 955 by 366 using the standard method:\n\nFirst, write it down as:\n\n      955\n    \u00d7 366\n    -------\n\nWe can break this into three parts: multiplying by 6 (units place), 60 (tens place), and 300 (hundreds place), then add them up.\n\nStarting with the units digit (6):\n\n955 \u00d7 6 = ?\n\nLet's compute that:\n\n900 \u00d76 = 5,400\n\n50\u00d76 = 300\n\n5\u00d76 = 30\n\nAdding those: 5,400 + 300 = 5,700; 5,700 +30 =5,730. So that's 5,730.\n\nNext, the tens place (60). So 955 \u00d760. Since it's the tens place, we can compute 955\u00d76 and then add a zero.\n\n955 \u00d76 = 5,730, so 5,730 \u00d710 = 57,300.\n\nNow the hundreds place (300). 955\u00d7300. Similarly, compute 955\u00d73 and add two zeros.\n\n955 \u00d73: 900\u00d73=2,700; 50\u00d73=150; 5\u00d73=15. Adding those: 2,700+150=2,850; +15=2,865. So 2,865 \u00d7100 = 286,500.\n\nNow, add all three results together:\n\nFirst, 5,730 (units) + 57,300 (tens) = ?\n\n5,730 + 57,300. Let's see, 5,730 + 57,300. Adding the thousands: 5,000 + 57,000 = 62,000. Then 730 + 300 = 1,030. So total is 62,000 +1,030 = 63,030.\n\nThen add the hundreds place result: 63,030 + 286,500.\n\n63,030 + 286,500. Let's break it down:\n\n63,000 + 286,500 = 349,500. Then add the remaining 30: 349,500 +30 = 349,530.\n\nSame result! Okay, so that matches. So that's reassuring.\n\nWait, but just to make sure I didn't make a mistake in the breakdown, let me try another approach. Maybe using another method like the lattice method or something else? Hmm, but maybe overkill here. Alternatively, approximate the numbers to check if 349,530 is reasonable.\n\nEstimate: 955 is about 1000, and 366 is about 400. So 1000\u00d7400=400,000. Since both numbers are slightly less than the estimates, the actual product should be a bit less than 400,000. 349,530 is 50,470 less, which seems plausible. Alternatively, 955 is 45 less than 1000, so 1000\u00d7366 = 366,000 minus 45\u00d7366. Let's see:\n\n45\u00d7366: 45\u00d7300=13,500; 45\u00d760=2,700; 45\u00d76=270. So total is 13,500+2,700=16,200 +270=16,470. So 366,000 -16,470 = 349,530. Exactly! That's another way to see it. \n\nSo that confirms it again. So the answer must be 349,530. I think that's solid.\n\nJust to recap, breaking it down in different ways all lead to the same result, so I can be confident that the product is indeed 349,530.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 955 and 366 is \\boxed{349530}.\n<\uff5cUser\uff5c>\n<\uff5c\n\nOkay, so I need to figure out the product of 955 and 366. Hmm, let's see. I remember from school that multiplying large numbers can be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe using the distributive property? Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I should break down both numbers into their hundreds, tens, and ones. Let's start with 955. Breaking that down, it's 900 + 50 + 5. Right? Because 900 is the hundreds place, 50 is the tens, and 5 is the ones. \n\nNow 366. That would be 300 + 60 + 6. Yep, same logic here. 300 for the hundreds, 60 for the tens, and 6 for the ones.\n\nSo, if I use the distributive property, it's like (900 + 50 + 5) multiplied by (300 + 60 + 6). That should give me all the combinations of each part multiplied together. Let me write that out:\n\n(900 + 50 + 5) \u00d7 (300 + 60 + 6) \n\nExpanding this, I need to multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number. That sounds like a lot of terms, but maybe manageable. Let me list them all out.\n\nStarting with the 900 part:\n- 900 \u00d7 300 = ?\nHmm, 900 times 300. Well, 9 times 3 is 27, and then adding four zeros (since 900 is 9\u00d7100 and 300 is 3\u00d7100, so total 100\u00d7100 = 10,000). So 27\u00d710,000 = 270,000. Okay, that's straightforward.\n\nNext, 900 \u00d7 60. Let's see, 900 times 60. 9\u00d76 is 54, and then three zeros (since 100\u00d710 = 1000). So 54,000.\n\nThen, 900 \u00d7 6. That should be 900\u00d76. 9\u00d76 is 54, so 54\u00d7100 = 5,400. Got that.\n\nNow moving on to the 50 part:\n- 50 \u00d7 300. 5\u00d73 is 15, so 15\u00d7100\u00d710 = 15,000? Wait, no. Wait, 50\u00d7300 is 50\u00d73\u00d7100. 50\u00d73 is 150, so 150\u00d7100 is 15,000. Right.\n\n- 50 \u00d7 60. That's 50\u00d760. 5\u00d76=30, and then two zeros (since 50 is 5\u00d710 and 60 is 6\u00d710, so 10\u00d710=100). So 30\u00d7100=3,000.\n\n- 50 \u00d7 6. 50\u00d76 is 300. Yeah, 5\u00d76=30, so 30\u00d710=300.\n\nNow the 5 part:\n- 5 \u00d7 300. 5\u00d7300 is 1,500. Because 5\u00d73=15, so 15\u00d7100=1,500.\n\n- 5 \u00d7 60. That's 5\u00d760=300. 5\u00d76=30, so 30\u00d710=300.\n\n- 5 \u00d7 6. That's just 30. Easy enough.\n\nAlright, so now I have all the partial products. Let me list them again to make sure I didn't miss any:\n\nFrom 900:\n- 270,000\n- 54,000\n- 5,400\n\nFrom 50:\n- 15,000\n