\n\\]\n\\[\n790 \\times 2 = 1,580\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the second result from the first:\n\n\\[\n158,000 - 1,580 = 156,420\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we use direct multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n      790 \\\\\n\\times 198 \\\\\n\\hline\n      6,320 \\quad (\\text{790 × 8}) \\\\\n    71,100 \\quad (\\text{790 × 90, shifted left by 1}) \\\\\n+ 79,000 \\quad (\\text{790 × 100, shifted left by 2}) \\\\\n\\hline\n    156,420 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. Additionally, an approximation check using rounded values (800 × 200 = 160,000) confirms the answer is reasonable.\n\nThe product of 790 and 198 is \\(\\boxed{156420}\\). <｜End｜> <｜WaitStop｜>\nOkay, I need to multiply 790 by 198. Let me start by breaking down 198 into 200 minus 2. That should make the calculation easier. \n\nFirst, calculate 790 times 200. Since 790 times 2 is 1,580, multiplying by 200 (which is 2 times 100) gives 1,580 times 100, so that's 158,000. \n\nNext, subtract 790 multiplied by 2. That's 1,580. \n\nSo subtracting 1,580 from 158,000: 158,000 minus 1,000 is 157,000. Then subtract another 580 to get 156,420. \n\nLet me check using another method. Multiply 790 by 198 directly:\n\n- Multiply 790 by 8: 790*8 = 6,320\n- Multiply 790 by 90 (shifted one place): 790*9=7,110 → 71,100\n- Multiply 790 by 100 (shifted two places): 79,000\n\nAdding them: 6,320 + 71,100 = 77,420. Then add 79,000 to get 156,420. \n\nBoth methods give the same result. Approximating with 800*200=160,000, the answer is a bit less, which matches. \n\nSo, the final answer is 156,420.\n\n\\boxed{156420} <｜End｜>\n\nTo find the product of 790 and 198, we use the distributive property by expressing 198 as \\(200 - 2\\):\n\n\\[\n790 \\times 198 = 790 \\times (200 - 2)\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n= 790 \\times 200 - 790 \\times 2\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n790 \\times 200 = 158,000 \\quad \\text{(since } 790 \\times 2 = 1,580 \\text{ and } 1,580 \\times 100 = 158,000\\tex