      "text": "88,830.\n<\uff5c/Assistant\uff5c>\n\nAlright, so I need to figure out the product of 846 and 105. Hmm, let me think. I remember that multiplying larger numbers can sometimes be broken down into smaller parts to make it easier. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. \n\nFirst, I'll try breaking down 105 into something simpler. 105 is 100 plus 5, right? So if I do 846 times (100 + 5), that should work. Let me write that out: 846 \u00d7 100 plus 846 \u00d7 5. \n\nStarting with the first part, 846 multiplied by 100. Well, multiplying by 100 is straightforward; you just add two zeros to the end of the number. So 846 becomes 84600. Got that part down. \n\nNow the second part: 846 times 5. Hmm, 800 times 5 is 4000, and 46 times 5 is 230. Adding those together gives 4000 + 230 = 4230. Wait, let me check that again. 846 \u00d7 5: 800\u00d75 is indeed 4000, and 40\u00d75 is 200, plus 6\u00d75 is 30. So 4000 + 200 + 30 = 4230. Yep, that's right. \n\nNow I have to add those two results together: 84600 + 4230. Let me add them step by step. 84600 plus 4000 is 88600, and then adding the remaining 230 gives 88600 + 230 = 88830. \n\nWait a second, let me verify that addition again. 84600 + 4230. If I break it down, 84600 + 4000 = 88600, then adding 230 more: 88600 + 200 is 88800, and then +30 is 88830. Yep, that checks out. \n\nJust to make sure I didn't make a mistake, maybe I can try another method. Let's do the multiplication the standard way, writing it out vertically. \n\n       846\n     \u00d7 105\n     -------\nFirst, multiply 846 by 5: that's 4230. Then multiply 846 by 0 (the tens place of 105, which is 0), so that would be 0, but shifted one position to the left. Then multiply 846 by 1 (the hundreds place of 105, which is 100), so 846 \u00d7 100 = 84600. Wait, actually, in standard multiplication, the 1 in 105 is in the hundreds place, so it's 100, so when you write it out:\n\n       846\n     \u00d7 105\n     -------\n        4230  (this is 846 \u0